    <h2>Current initialization path</h2>

    <p>
        <code>GameAPIs.Initialize()</code> scans the game's Assets directory, creates <code>BlockRegistry</code> and <code>ItemRegistry</code>, builds a <code>ModContext</code> containing a logger and those registries, then constructs <code>MainMod</code> and calls <code>Initilaize</code>. The built-in mod registers the game's content.
    </p>

    <h2>What is not in the checked-in path</h2>

    <ul class="docs-list">
        <li>Scanning a mods directory for third-party assemblies.</li>
        <li>Loading external mod assemblies and locating their IMod implementations.</li>
        <li>Dependency ordering, version compatibility checks, or conflict resolution.</li>
        <li>Mod isolation, unload, or hot reload.</li>
        <li>A defined external mod package format or install location.</li>
        <li>A working MDK template that generates a mod project.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>About planned architecture notes</h2>

    <p>
        The repository README lists first-class modding, multithreaded loading, and hot reload among design principles, but its known-gaps section explicitly says the loader is not present in this code. The implementation status must take precedence over aspirational design notes when documenting what developers can use today.
    </p>

    <h2>What a future loader needs to define</h2>

    <ul class="docs-list">
        <li>Mod discovery and package/manifest format.</li>
        <li>Assembly loading strategy and dependency resolution.</li>
        <li>Initialization order and duplicate ID behavior.</li>
        <li>Asset path resolution and content ownership.</li>
        <li>Failure handling, logging, and compatibility rules.</li>
        <li>Unload/reload semantics and resource cleanup.</li>
        <li>Public SDK compatibility policy and supported extension points.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="docs-callout docs-callout-info">
        <i class="bi bi-info-circle"></i>

        <div>
            <strong>Practical guidance</strong>

            <p>
                You can study the built-in <code>MainMod</code> as an example of registering content against the SDK interfaces. You cannot yet follow a complete, supported workflow to install an independently compiled third-party mod into the game using this repository snapshot alone.
            </p>
        </div>
